/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  0/diluateFeed -- THE WINE, as an ionic composition at 3000 L/h (the stack's
  design flow).  EVERY CONCENTRATION BELOW IS AN AUTHOR-SET ASSUMPTION --
  round numbers of the order a dry red wine at pH about 3.6 carries, NOT
  transcribed from any measured wine and not derived from a speciation this
  case performs (the stack does not speciate; see system/flowsheetDict):

      ion        mmol/L   z     meq/L
      K+         25.0     +1    25.0
      Na+         5.0     +1     5.0
      Ca2+        1.25    +2     2.5
      Mg2+        4.0     +2     8.0
      H+          0.25    +1     0.25     (pH 3.6 written as a concentration)
      Cl-         1.1     -1     1.1
      SO4 2-      6.025   -2    12.05     THE CLOSURE ION, see below
      HTart-      8.0     -1     8.0      hydrogen tartrate
      Tart 2-     1.5     -2     3.0      tartrate
      HMal-       1.0     -1     1.0      hydrogen malate
      Mal 2-      0.05    -2     0.1      malate
      Lactate-    9.0     -1     9.0
      H2PO4-      6.5     -1     6.5

  ELECTRONEUTRALITY IS CLOSED ON SULFATE, and the closure is stated, not
  hidden: the cations sum to 40.75 meq/L and the other anions to 28.70, so
  6.025 mmol/L of SO4 2- carries the 12.05 meq/L gap.  That is more sulfate
  than a wine should hold; the gap really stands for the anions this
  composition does not declare (succinate, acetate and others a wine
  carries), and the reader may move it.  The undissociated acids carry no
  current and are not declared at all.

  The water is 3000 kg/h, so that the engine's carrier volume (rho 1000
  kg/m3, the dilute-carrier approximation the unit announces) equals the
  wine's 3000 L/h and every mmol/L above is what the stack sees.  The real
  wine holds less water -- the rest is ETHANOL, which cannot be declared
  (system/flowsheetDict, first paragraph).
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

